able store** — the other two\n398-  adapters read append-only transcript files, so `conversation.seq` (the\n399-  source line index) is stable forever and event ids never churn. opencode\n400-  keeps sessions in SQLite, where `session revert`, `message.removed` and\n401-  `part.removed` can delete parts out of the middle of a session. Deleting a\n402-  part shifts the positions of everything after it, and since `seq` is part of\n403-  event identity, the shifted parts re-capture under new ids — duplicates in\n404-  the ledger rather than lost or corrupted content, and only for sessions you\n405-  actually revert. Capturing per *part* rather than per message already keeps\n406-  the blast radius to the parts after the deletion. A real fix would derive\n407-  `seq` from opencode's own part ids instead of a running index, which needs\n408-  those ids to be reliably orderable; worth confirming before committing to\n409-  it. Related: opencode's `session delete` removes a conversation from\n410-  opencode entirely while the ledger keeps it, which is a feature, not a bug.\n411-- **The opencode hook's session-id fallback is untested** — the plugin reads\n412-  `event.properties.sessionID`, confirmed against a live `session.idle` from\n413-  opencode 1.18.5, so the normal path is pinned to the real field. The hook\n414-  still treats the id as optional and falls back to capturing the most\n415-  recently updated session for the directory, so a future opencode that\n416-  renames the field degrades instead of silently stopping. That fallback has\n417-  never run in anger, and it would pick the wrong session if two sessions in\n418-  one project went idle in the same instant. Worth a test that exercises it\n419-  directly, and a warning when it triggers, so a silent rename does not look\n420-  like normal operation.\n421-- **`opencode export` truncates a piped stdout at 64KB** — an upstream Bun\n422-  flush bug: the process exits without draining stdout, so any session over\n423-  ~64KB comes back cut off, and the truncated JSON can still parse. cledger\n424-  works around it by routing the child's stdout to a temp file rather than a\n425-  pipe (see `runOpencode`). Worth reporting upstream; the workaround should\n426-  stay regardless, since it costs nothing and protects older opencode\n427-  versions.\n428-- **Config sections replace rather than merge, and it fails open** — *fixed in\n429-  0.21.0*: `loadConfig` now merges each top-level section key-by-key, so a\n430-  repo `.cledger.json` that sets only `redact.patterns` no longer silently\n431-  reverts a globally-enabled `redact.knownSecrets` to its default. The one\n432-  deliberate exception: `scan.allowFingerprints` arrays are unioned rather\n433-  than repo-wins, because the allowlist is accumulative — a fingerprint either\n434-  config trusts is trusted, and replacement would make a repo-local entry\n435-  silently drop every personal one. Kept here rather than deleted because the\n436-  bug is a family member (see below) and its shape — a config surface where\n437-  *mentioning* a section disabled a sibling protection — is worth remembering\n438-  when adding config keys.\n439-\n440-  **Self-referential failures are now this project's characteristic bug\n441-  family**, and are worth a standing check rather than four independent\n442-  fixes. cledger records the work of building cledger, so its own state,\n443-  output, and documentation feed back into its behavior. Known members: the\n444-  capture/scan redaction loop (a finding report re-seeding the finding it\n445-  describes — closed in 0.6.1/0.7.0); the pre-0.13.0 worktree state split\n446-  (0.13.0); this config section-replace; and the allowlist entry below, whose\n447-  *write-up* of a false positive reproduces the false positive in every repo\n448-  whose conversations quote it. Three of the four fail silently, and two fail\n449-  open. A useful tripwire when adding anything that reads cledger's own\n450-  state or prints cledger's own findings: ask what happens when that output\n451-  is itself captured.\n452-- **Don't print findings by default; print a pointer to them** — the cleanest\n453-  structural break in the self-referential loop above. Today `cledger scan`\n454-  and the sync gate print every finding inline, which is exactly the text that\n455-  gets captured into the next conversation and re-seeds the finding. Masking\n456-  the matched span (0.6.1) removed the secret characters but not the\n457-  *surrounding* credential-shaped context, which is what\n458-  `keyword-assignment` trips on — so the loop is narrowed, not closed.\n459-  Proposed: report only the count and how to inspect, with the audience split\n460-  made explicit in the text — a human is told which command reveals the\n461-  findings; an agent is told not to run it, because reading the findings into\n462-  its context is what reproduces them, and to surface the count to its human\n463-  instead. Naming the failure mode in the output is the point: an agent that\n464-  understands *why* will comply, where a bare \"don't look\" invites a\n465-  workaround. Pairs naturally with the inspection command in the design doc's\n466-  open questions, which already has to write unmasked output to a file rather\n467-  than stdout for the same reason. *Tension to resolve first:* CI reads those\n468-  printed findings, and `cledger scan`'s nonzero exit plus inline report is\n469-  the documented CI integration — so the default likely has to be\n470-  terminal-aware, or CI has to opt in explicitly (`--report`), rather than\n471-  simply going quiet for everyone.\n472-- **Branch-scoped push** *(shipped, 0.14.0)* — reachability filtered *reads*\n473-  (`cledger log`, `show`, `conversations`), but the notes ref is a single ref\n474-  and a ref push sends all of it, so conversations from a branch that was\n475-  abandoned and never merged still reached `origin` and landed on every\n476-  collaborator's disk. Two scopes wearing one name, with push the odd one out:\n477-  storage was already per-commit and reads already per-branch.\n478-\n479-  Push now carries only the anchors a read at that rev resolves to. Storage is\n480-  unchanged (commit-anchored, one local ref, one shared remote ref) — only\n481-  push learns the granularity storage already had. `cledger sync` scopes to\n482-  the current branch by default, `--rev` scopes elsewhere, `--all` restores\n483-  the whole-ledger push. **No mapping is stored anywhere:** reachability is\n484-  recomputed at push time from the DAG, the same reason reads never stored it.\n485-\n486-  *Squash merges are the case that decides the implementation.* The scope\n487-  reuses `resolveAnchors` — the same function reads use — rather than a plain\n488-  `git rev-list`. A squash discards the source branch's commits, so its\n489-  conversations sit on anchors unreachable from the target, and only the\n490-  `re_anchor` mapping events (anchored to the *surviving* commit) tie them\n491-  back. A rev-list-only scope would push those mapping events while leaving\n492-  behind the events they point at — dropping exactly what re-anchoring exists\n493-  to save. Covered by a test that fails against a rev-list-only scope.\n494-\n495-  *The destructive shortcut, recorded because it is the natural first\n496-  implementation.* A notes ref is a snapshot of the whole database, not an\n497-  append log: its tip tree maps every annotated commit to its note, so\n498-  whatever tree you push becomes the remote's entire notes database. Filtering\n499-  your local ref down to one branch and pushing that still fast-forwards — and\n500-  silently deletes every other branch's conversations from the remote's\n501-  current view (they survive in the ref's history but vanish from `git notes\n502-  show` and from any fresh clone). The scoped push therefore seeds a temp ref\n503-  from the remote's tip and unions its scope *into* that, so the push strictly\n504-  adds. Also covered by a test that fails against the filtered-tree version.\n505-\n506-  *Side benefit:* the pre-push scan gate now inspects only what the push would\n507-  carry, so a finding in a branch that is not being pushed no longer blocks\n508-  shipping unrelated work.\n509-\n510-  *Scope follows the refs actually being pushed* (0.15.0). The hook is fed\n511-  git's pre-push stdin — one line per ref — so `git push origin feat` from\n512-  another branch shares feat's conversations. 0.14.0 shipped with `</dev/null`\n513-  and scoped by `HEAD`, which shared nothing in that case. Ref deletions are\n514-  skipped (no tip to scope to), multiple refs union, and anything unusable\n515-  falls back to `HEAD` rather than to the whole ledger. Because this changes\n516-  the installed hook, `installHook` now replaces a stale cledger block in\n517-  place instead of leaving any file containing the marker untouched — only the\n518-  marked region is rewritten, so surrounding user content survives.\n519-\n520-  *Not addressed here:* the read default. `cledger export` still defaults to\n521-  no reachability filter, and the fix belongs in `readEvents` rather than in\n522-  one CLI command, since library consumers (intent-recall) never go through\n523-  the CLI and would otherwise inherit the unscoped view. Separate entry below.\n524-  Per-branch remote refs (`…-branches/<branch>` plus a wildcard fetch refspec)\n525-  were considered and are *not* needed; they would only add fetch-side\n526-  selectivity, letting a collaborator decline a branch's conversations\n527-  entirely. Worth revisiting only if that is ever wanted.\n528-- **Reads default to unscoped** — `readEvents` applies reachability only when\n529-  the caller asks, and `cledger export` never asks, so an automated consumer\n530-  reading the ledger directly gets every branch's conversations unless it\n531-  knows to pass a rev. Now that push is scoped, this is the remaining half of\n532-  the same mismatch. The default belon