ger-redaction-state\ndescription: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (through 0.21.0 review UX), decisions made, and what's deferred\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df\n  modified: 2026-08-16T00:00:00.000Z\n---\n\nState of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-08-16.\n\n**2026-08-16 — 0.21.0 redaction *review UX* overhaul, MERGED to main by the user, installed (npm-link rebuild), both live queues cleared.** Motivation: 153 finding sites were really 11 decisions; review meant leaving the CLI for a .txt file and retyping the secret as a regex; allowlists were per-repo so decisions didn't travel; agents kept minting fixture-shaped findings. Shipped:\n- `cledger review` TUI (`src/review.ts`, dependency-free ANSI): one screen per distinct fingerprint, ruled content pane with `│` gutter, match highlighted, bracketed-key verdict menu — [a] allow repo / [g] allow global / [r] redact (span auto-escaped to a literal pattern, human never retypes it; re-scans after each redact since offsets shift) / [s] skip. Refuses in agent sessions (no --force, unlike inspect) and without TTYs on both ends. Layout iterated once on user feedback: chrome/content separation + menu-not-sentence.\n- Reports group by fingerprint everywhere; `cledger scan` stdout keeps line-per-site for CI.\n- Allowlist tiers unioned at scan: per-repo `.git/…/allowlist.json`, per-user `~/.config/cledger/allowlist.json` (`allow --global`), committable `scan.allowFingerprints` in `.cledger.json`.\n- Fixture-marker convention: scan-tier rules skip spans containing uppercase FAKE/EXAMPLE/PLACEHOLDER/DUMMY/NOTREAL/TESTONLY; capture-tier deliberately ignores markers. Repo `CLAUDE.md` (new) teaches agents to author fixtures this way; format-valid tokens stay split-parts in the corpus.\n- Enforcement audit: config sections now merge per-key (old per-section replacement failed open — repo `.cledger.json` touching `redact.patterns` silently killed global `knownSecrets`; allowFingerprints arrays union rather than repo-wins); `inAgentSession` gained OPENCODE/GEMINI_CLI/QWEN markers.\n- README \"What cledger does and does not protect\": the one enforced guarantee is the pre-push gate on the ledger ref; local disk, agent-readable secrets, pasted secrets, and export/log/show are explicitly out of scope; post-push remedy is rotate-the-credential.\n\n**Considered and deferred with evidence (2026-08-16, in README roadmap — don't re-propose without new data):** auto-triage of findings whose span exists in committed repo content. Built the prover; it cleared **0 of the 15 live findings** (split-parts corpus keeps assembled bytes out of committed files; other spans were shell commands / intermediate edit states). Also deferred: conversation-paging in the reviewer (per-span context has sufficed so far).\n\n**Fingerprint provenance trick (reusable):** a finding fingerprint is `sha256(matchedSpan).slice(0,12)`, so an agent can prove things about flagged content without reading it — hash candidate spans from committed blobs / reassembled fixtures and intersect, or extract only-safe metadata (tool name, file_path) from the flagged events. Identical fingerprints across repos = byte-identical spans, so one human verdict transfers. Also: the permission classifier blocks agents from running `cledger allow` — correct; hand the human ready-made commands instead.","oldString":"---\nname: project-cledger-redaction-state\ndescription: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (0.6.1/0.7.0), decisions made, and what's deferred\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df\n  modified: 2026-08-03T04:10:18.432Z\n---\n\nState of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-08-03.","originalFile":"---\nname: project-cledger-redaction-state\ndescription: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (0.6.1/0.7.0), decisions made, and what's deferred\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df\n  modified: 2026-08-03T04:10:18.432Z\n---\n\nState of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-08-03.\n\n**2026-08-03 — redaction reframed as pre-push-only. MERGED to main as 0.18.0 (PR #3), after PR #2's opencode adapter landed as 0.17.0. Global `npm link` installation rebuilt; opencode plugin refreshed.**\nFour fixes, all verified end-to-end against real repos/remotes, not just unit tests:\n- `cledger redact` now **refuses** when the target event is already on origin, or when origin is unreachable (fails closed — an offline `ls-remote` used to look identical to a virgin remote). Gating is **per event**, not per repo, so routine pushing doesn't kill redaction for later captures. It previously warned and proceeded, which was worse than useless: `pushScopedNotes` seeds from the remote's tip and unions the local scope in, so redact-then-push **re-uploaded** the original next to the rewrite.\n- `dedupById` now consults **redaction depth first**. Redacted event + original share an id by design *and* share `recorded_at` (the rewrite spreads the original), so both ordering keys tied and a canonical-JSON byte compare decided — resolving on the secret's first char vs `[` (0x5B). Every secret starting with a digit or uppercase letter won, so reads printed plaintext beside the `redaction` event claiming removal.\n- Output no longer says `history squashed: yes` (severing the notes chain ≠ object purge; residue is local-only since git transfers neither reflogs nor unreachable objects).\n- Malformed-but-present config is now reported instead of silently reverting all settings to default.\n\nKey structural insight, worth not re-deriving: **the notes ref carries its own commit chain**, so every prior note body reaches every clone and is recoverable with `git log -p refs/notes/conversation-ledger` — combined with `cat_sort_uniq` unioning *lines*, this makes true deletion impossible on this substrate. That's why redaction is now scoped to pre-push only and post-push removal is explicitly a separate destructive tool that doesn't exist. `docs/SCRATCH-redaction-does-not-redact.md` was deleted (resolved); its reflog finding was correct but local-only, and it missed the severe post-push re-upload path.\n\n**Shipped earlier:**\n- 0.6.1 — scan/sync finding reports mask the matched span. **Superseded by 0.16.0**: masking the match wasn't enough, because the *context* around it is what tripped the rule, so reports re-seeded findings on themselves. Reports now print **coordinates only** (event id, rule, path@offset, fingerprint); readable output moved to `cledger inspect`, which writes a 0600 file outside the repo and refuses to run inside an agent session.\n- 0.7.0 — opt-in `redact.knownSecrets` (default off). `cledger redact --pattern` remembers the exact scrubbed plaintext in `.git/conversation-ledger/known-secrets.json` (local, git-invisible, beside the allowlist); capture-time redaction then exact-matches those values out of every future draft under a `known-secret` rule id. Only `--pattern` feeds it (`--all` blanks whole content); sub-8-char values dropped. Enabled globally on this machine via `~/.config/cledger/config.json`.\n- 0.7.1 — `keyword-assignment` scan rule tightened (value charset excludes code punctuation, killing the type-annotation / template-interpolation false positives; this repo went 93 -> 64 findings); known-secrets store written `0600` with perm repair on each write; tests now redirect HOME to a throwaway dir so the developer's global config can't change test outcomes.\n\n**Decisions (so the next agent doesn't re-litigate):**\n- The roadmap's original \"make capture-tier a strict superset of scan-tier\" framing was considered and **rejected** — promoting the deliberately-noisy scan rules to run at capture would silently rewrite innocent text, breaking the near-zero-false-positive invariant. The value-based learning above is the intended narrow form.\n- Store holds plaintext by necessity (fingerprints are one-way, can't drive exact matching); acceptable because `.git/` is as local-and-unshared as the transcripts, per the transport trust boundary.\n\n**Deferred, roughly by urgency:**\n1. **Hash the known-secrets store instead of encrypting it** — decided 2026-08-03, roadmap entry written, not built. Store `{salted sha256, byte length}` rather than plaintext: removes the secret entirely (no key to manage, nothing recoverable even by cledger), superseding the older encrypt-with-OS-keychain plan. Open design point is candidate generation, since you can't search for a value you can't reconstruct: (a) run broad scan-tier rules as candidate *generators* and confirm by hash — cheap, and their noise is harmless because the hash gate is exact, but blind to values no rule recognizes, which is exactly what the store exists for; (b) length-indexed Rabin–Karp rolling sweep — linear, subsumes (a), more code. Accepted trade (user's call): low-entropy human passwords stay dictionary-attackable; this tool targets code repos where the realistic secret is a generated token.\n2. Post-push purge, re-anchor, path-based capture exclusion, auto-renormalize, harness-artifact capture, sub-turn citation anchors.\n\n**Dogfooding note:** this repo captures its own sessions. It once had ~64 benign scan findings (fixtures containing literal `password=supersecret123`-style strings, which no pattern can distinguish from real secrets) that held the ledger push back; as of 2026-07-26 `cledger scan` on this repo reports **no findings** — the allowlist has absorbed them, so the ledger push is no longer blocked. The open question this leaves is the roadmap's \"audit the allowlist for generalizable false-positive patterns\" item: allowlisting is per-repo, so every downstream consumer whose codebase talks *about* secrets hits the same `keyword-assignment` false positives independently.\n\nRelated: [[project-ledger-ecosystem]]\n","replaceAll":false,"structuredPatch":[{"lines":[" ---"," name: project-cledger-redaction-state","-description: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (0.6.1/0.7.0), decisions made, and what's deferred\"","+description: \"conversation-ledger secret-redaction stack — what shipped (through 0.21.0 review UX), decisions made, and what's deferred\""," metadata: ","   node_type: memory","   type: project","   originSessionId: 48648da3-5c2c-4827-96d5-7a6ed13224df","-  modified: 2026-08-03T04:10:18.432Z","+  modified: 2026-08-16T10:08:44.689Z"," ---"," ","-State of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-08-03.","+State of conversation-ledger's redaction work as of 2026-08-16."," ","+**2026-08-16 — 0.21.0 redaction *review UX* overhaul, MERGED to main by the user, installed (npm-link rebuild), both live queues cleared.** Motivation: 153 finding sites were really 11 decisions; review meant leaving the CLI for a .txt file and retyping the secret as a regex; allowlists were per-repo so decisions didn't travel; agents kept minting fixture-shaped findings. Shipped:","+- `cledger review` TUI (`src/review.ts`, dependency-free ANSI): one screen per distinct fingerprint, ruled content pane with `│` gutter, match highlighted, bracketed-key verdict menu — [a] allow repo / [g] allow global / [r] redact (span auto-escaped to a literal pattern, human never retypes it; re-scans after each redact since offsets shift) / [s] skip. Refuses in agent sessions (no --force, unlike inspect) and without TTYs on both ends. Layout iterated once on user feedback: chrome/content separation + menu-not-sentence.","+- Reports group by fingerprint everywhere; `cledger scan` stdout keeps line-per-site for CI.","+- Allowlist tiers unioned at scan: per-repo `.git/…/allowlist.json`, per-user `~/.config/cledger/allowlist.json` (`allow --global`), committable `scan.allowFingerprints` in `.cledger.json`.","+- Fixture-marker conventio